  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Finding the Support of $x(\\tau)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Identify the nonzero interval of $x(\\tau)$ in the convolution variable so it becomes the fixed support constraint for determining overlap with $h(t-\\tau)$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding the Support of $x(\\tau)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Finding the Support of $x(\\tau)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In a convolution integral, $\\tau$ is the integration variable. The value of $t$ is treated as fixed later, but the first support constraint comes only from the signal $x(\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The support of $x(\\tau)$ means the set of $\\tau$ values where $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero. To find it, ignore $h(t-\\tau)$ for now and read only where the input-side signal is active.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x=\\{\\tau: x(\\tau)\\ne 0\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the cleanest case, suppose $x(\\tau)$ is equal to $1$ on one interval and is $0$ everywhere else. The support is exactly the interval where the nonzero branch appears.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=\\begin{cases}1,&0\\le \\tau\\le 2\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 0\\le \\tau\\le 2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fixed support constraint from $x(\\tau)$ is $0\\le \\tau\\le 2$. It is fixed because it depends only on $x(\\tau)$ and contains no $t$; later overlap work will combine this fixed constraint with the moving support of $h(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding the Support of $x(\\tau)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Reading the Support of a Basic Signal in $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the support of $x(\\tau)$ if $x(\\tau)=2$ for $-1\\le \\tau\\le 3$ and $x(\\tau)=0$ otherwise.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the phrase that tells us exactly when $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero. Here, $x(\\tau)=2$ on $-1\\le \\tau\\le 3$, so the amplitude $2$ only tells us the signal is active on that interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=2 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad -1\\le \\tau\\le 3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Everywhere outside that interval, the signal is given as $0$, so those $\\tau$ values are not part of the support. The support is the interval from the nonzero clause.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x=\\{\\tau:x(\\tau)\\ne0\\}=[-1,3]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fixed support constraint for this signal is $-1\\le \\tau\\le 3$. This constraint comes only from $x(\\tau)$; no comparison with $h(t-\\tau)$ is needed in this step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding the Support of $x(\\tau)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding Support from a Piecewise Definition in $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the support of $x(\\tau)=\\begin{cases}0,&\\tau<-1\\\\\\tau+3,&-1\\le \\tau\\le 2\\\\0,&\\tau>2\\end{cases}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the piecewise definition. To find the support, scan the branches and separate the branches where $x(\\tau)$ is exactly $0$ from the branch where $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=\\begin{cases}0,&\\tau<-1\\\\\\tau+3,&-1\\le \\tau\\le 2\\\\0,&\\tau>2\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The first branch is zero for $\\tau<-1$, and the last branch is zero for $\\tau>2$. Those intervals are not part of the support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The remaining branch is $x(\\tau)=\\tau+3$ on $-1\\le \\tau\\le 2$. This is the active branch, so its condition gives the support interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x=\\{\\tau:x(\\tau)\\ne0\\}=[-1,2]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fixed support constraint for this signal is $-1\\le \\tau\\le 2$. This step uses only the branches of $x(\\tau)$; it does not involve $h(t-\\tau)$ or any overlap calculation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding the Support of $x(\\tau)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding Support from Unit Step Windows in $\\tau$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the support of $x(\\tau)=(u(\\tau-1)-u(\\tau-4))e^{-\\tau}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unit-step difference $u(\\tau-1)-u(\\tau-4)$. This factor acts like a window: it turns the signal on at one $\\tau$ value and turns it off at another.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=(u(\\tau-1)-u(\\tau-4))e^{-\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The term $u(\\tau-1)$ turns on at $\\tau=1$. The subtracted term $u(\\tau-4)$ turns on at $\\tau=4$, which turns the window off after that point.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(\\tau-1)-u(\\tau-4) \\quad \\text{selects the window} \\quad 1\\le \\tau\\le 4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The multiplier $e^{-\\tau}$ does not create a new cutoff because it is nonzero for every $\\tau$. So the support comes from the unit-step window.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x=\\{\\tau:x(\\tau)\\ne0\\}=[1,4]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fixed support constraint for this signal is $1\\le \\tau\\le 4$. This is still a constraint from $x(\\tau)$ only; no overlap with $h(t-\\tau)$ is being formed yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Finding the Support of $x(\\tau)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Writing the Fixed Support Constraint for $x(\\tau)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given that $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero only for $-2\\le \\tau\\le 1$, write the fixed support constraint for $x(\\tau)$ that will be used later when finding overlap with $h(t-\\tau)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the nonzero interval for $x(\\tau)$ has already been identified. Since $x(\\tau)$ is nonzero only for $-2\\le \\tau\\le 1$, that interval is the support of $x$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{supp} x=[-2,1]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To write this as a support constraint, keep the convolution variable $\\tau$ in the inequality and copy the support endpoints in increasing order.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-2\\le \\tau\\le 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This constraint is fixed because it contains no $t$. It comes only from $x(\\tau)$, so it does not move as the output time changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{fixed support constraint from }x(\\tau):\\quad -2\\le \\tau\\le 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Later overlap work will combine this fixed constraint with the support of $h(t-\\tau)$. For this step, stop here and do not form an intersection.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
